 \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"apatite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"phosphate-rich concentrated microphase or mineral interface; repeated heating/cooling without complete desiccation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The distal side-chain amine adds to cyanamide to form an amidino intermediate that tautomerizes to the guanidino group of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanamide is a plausible prebiotic amidinylating reagent. In a partially hydrated mineral-interface microphase, ornithine can be concentrated and oriented, while its alpha-amino group is partly deactivated by zwitterionic interactions relative to the distal side-chain amine. Regioselectivity is not guaranteed, but arginine formation is chemically plausible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-state cyanamide guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"geothermal dry-down residue or hot-spring margin with low water activity\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Low water activity concentrates ornithine and cyanamide, promoting amidination of the terminal amine to generate the guanidino functionality of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Dry-down conditions help overcome the entropic cost of guanidination by strongly concentrating reactants. This route is chemically direct and consistent with cyanamide-driven condensation chemistry, although side reactions and imperfect regioselectivity remain real uncertainties.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise dinitrile route with vent hydrolysis and interfacial guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrolein is made by aldol chemistry, trapped as 4-cyanobutanal, converted stepwise to the C5 aminodinitrile, reduced in a vent to ornithine nitrile, hydrolyzed hydrothermally to ornithine, and amidinated by cyanamide at a mineral interface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise dinitrile route with vent hydrolysis and dry-state guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"The same stepwise dinitrile branch is followed by hydrothermal hydrolysis to ornithine, after which cyanamide amidination occurs in a low-water geothermal residue.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise dinitrile route with surface hydrolysis and interfacial guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"After stepwise formation of the C5 dinitrile and hydrothermal reduction to ornithine nitrile, hydrolysis occurs during surface wet-dry cycling before cyanamide guanidination at a mineral interface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise dinitrile route with surface hydrolysis and dry-state guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"The stepwise acrolein -> 4-cyanobutanal -> dinitrile branch is completed by hydrothermal reduction, surface hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile, and dry-state cyanamide guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile chain-extension route with vent hydrolysis and interfacial guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Aminoacetonitrile is formed separately from formaldehyde, then chain-extended with acrolein to a C5 amino aldehyde nitrile, reductively aminated in a vent to ornithine nitrile, hydrolyzed hydrothermally, and guanidinated at a mineral interface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile chain-extension route with vent hydrolysis and dry-state guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"The aminoacetonitrile/acrolein branch is followed by hydrothermal reductive amination to ornithine nitrile, vent hydrolysis to ornithine, and dry-state cyanamide guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile chain-extension route with surface hydrolysis and interfacial guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"After acrolein chain extension and hydrothermal reductive amination, ornithine nitrile is hydrolyzed on a wet-dry surface and then converted to arginine by cyanamide at a mineral interface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile chain-extension route with surface hydrolysis and dry-state guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"The aminoacetonitrile branch reaches ornithine nitrile by hydrothermal reductive amination, then proceeds through surface hydrolysis and dry cyanamide amidination to arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot acrolein cascade with vent hydrolysis and interfacial guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrolein enters a one-pot cyanide/ammonia cascade to the C5 dinitrile without isolating 4-cyanobutanal; hydrothermal reduction gives ornithine nitrile, vent hydrolysis yields ornithine, and cyanamide at a mineral interface gives arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot acrolein cascade with surface hydrolysis and dry-state guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"The one-pot acrolein/HCN/NH3 cascade generates the C5 dinitrile, which is reduced hydrothermally to ornithine nitrile, hydrolyzed in a wet-dry surface setting, and amidinated by cyanamide under dry geothermal conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine", "overall_strategy": "The network builds the C5 ornithine scaffold from simple HCN-rich feedstocks and then installs the guanidino group with cyanamide. Three alternative scaffold-building routes converge on ornithine nitrile: (i) stepwise acrolein -> 4-cyanobutanal -> dinitrile chemistry, (ii) aminoacetonitrile chain extension with acrolein followed by reductive amination, and (iii) a one-pot acrolein/HCN/NH3 cascade. Surface chemistry is used for carbon-carbon and carbon-nitrogen bond formation, H2-rich hydrothermal minerals are used for the key reduction steps to ornithine nitrile, and ornithine is obtained by either hydrothermal or wet-dry hydrolysis before cyanamide-mediated guanidination in concentrated prebiotic microphases. The expected product is racemic arginine unless later chiral selection occurs.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C3H4O", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enal", "common_name": "acrolein", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H5NO", "iupac_name": "4-oxobutanenitrile", "common_name": "4-cyanobutanal", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentane-1,5-dinitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanenitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "5-oxo-2-aminopentanenitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H11N3", "iupac_name": "2,5-diaminopentanenitrile", "common_name": "ornithine nitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H12N2O2", "iupac_name": "2,5-diaminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "ornithine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "cyanamide", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C6H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-(diaminomethylideneamino)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "arginine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Cross-aldol condensation to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite", "aqueous": true, "other": "evaporitic pool or geothermal shoreline; wet-dry cycles promote dehydration of the initial aldol adduct; mildly basic due to dissolved ammonia/carbonate"}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde enolization, aldol addition to formaldehyde, and dehydration furnish the alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde acrolein.", "reasoning": "Simple carbonyl condensation is favored during evaporation on clay surfaces. Acrolein is a strategically valuable C3 electrophile because it supports both cyanide Michael addition and aminonitrile chain-extension chemistry under plausible prebiotic surface conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide Michael addition to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanide-bearing aqueous film; mildly basic conditions provide some CN- from HCN"}, "mechanism": "Conjugate 1,4-addition of cyanide to acrolein followed by protonation gives 4-cyanobutanal.", "reasoning": "Alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehydes are strong acceptors for cyanide under mild conditions. The product is a bifunctional intermediate that contains both an aldehyde for Strecker chemistry and a distal nitrile that can later become the ornithine side-chain amine."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Strecker homologation of 4-cyanobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "ammonium cyanide-rich brine or evaporitic shoreline film"}, "mechanism": "The aldehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine/iminium species, which is trapped by cyanide to form the alpha-aminonitrile while retaining the terminal nitrile.", "reasoning": "This is a straightforward Strecker-type extension from a C4 aldehyde nitrile to a C5 aminodinitrile. Chemically, this is one of the strongest links in the network because aldehydes, ammonia, and cyanide readily converge to aminonitriles under prebiotic conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Hydrothermal side-chain nitrile reduction to ornithine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["awaruite", "mackinawite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "awaruite/mackinawite", "aqueous": true, "other": "H2-rich alkaline vent pores; pH 9-10; requires transfer of the dinitrile into a reducing subsurface setting"}, "mechanism": "Hydrogen is activated on Ni-Fe mineral surfaces and used to reduce the terminal nitrile to a primary amine, giving ornithine nitrile.", "reasoning": "H2-rich hydrothermal minerals provide one of the most plausible prebiotic sources of reduction chemistry. The key uncertainty is chemoselectivity: the side-chain nitrile must be reduced while the alpha-aminonitrile survives long enough to exit the pore system. This makes the step a genuine bottleneck and partly speculative, but it is chemically meaningful as a direct route to the ornithine skeleton."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "ammonium cyanide solution or concentrated pool"}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde forms an imine/iminium with ammonia, and cyanide addition generates aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Aminoacetonitrile is among the most plausible and best-known prebiotic aminonitriles. In this network it functions as a masked glycine equivalent that can participate in later chain extension."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Aminonitrile chain extension with acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite", "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly basic concentrating shoreline film"}, "mechanism": "A carbon-centered nucleophilic equivalent derived from aminoacetonitrile adds to the beta-carbon of acrolein, forming a C5 amino aldehyde nitrile.", "reasoning": "Aminonitriles can behave as masked alpha-amino carbanion equivalents in concentrating environments. Direct evidence for this exact substrate pair is limited, so this branch is more speculative than classical Strecker chemistry, but it offers a chemically coherent C-C bond-forming route to the ornithine carbon skeleton."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrothermal reductive amination to ornithine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["awaruite", "mackinawite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "awaruite/mackinawite", "aqueous": true, "other": "subseafloor or vent-proximal H2-bearing fluids with dissolved ammonia"}, "mechanism": "The terminal aldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, and surface-activated hydrogen reduces it to the terminal primary amine, giving ornithine nitrile.", "reasoning": "This branch avoids selective reduction of one nitrile within a dinitrile and may therefore be more tractable than the direct dinitrile-reduction route. Reductive amination is chemically plausible where H2, ammonia, and Ni-Fe mineral surfaces coexist."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "One-pot acrolein/HCN/NH3 cascade to the C5 dinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "290-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanide-rich evaporitic pool; intermediates not isolated"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide first adds conjugatively to acrolein to generate 4-cyanobutanal in situ, after which the aldehyde undergoes Strecker capture with ammonia and cyanide.", "reasoning": "Prebiotic surface chemistry would often proceed as cascades rather than as isolated laboratory steps. If acrolein, ammonia, and cyanide coexist in the same pool, direct one-pot formation of the C5 aminodinitrile is chemically credible."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrothermal hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "380-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "continuous hot aqueous flow with local pH gradients across vent pores"}, "mechanism": "The alpha-aminonitrile is hydrated to the amide and then hydrolyzed to the carboxylic acid, yielding ornithine.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of aminonitriles to amino acids is the classical termination step of Strecker chemistry. Vent pores provide sustained hot water and acid-base microgradients that can accelerate conversion of ornithine nitrile to ornithine."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Surface wet-dry hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["apatite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "apatite", "aqueous": true, "other": "phosphate-rich shoreline crust subjected to repeated drying, heating, and rehydration"}, "mechanism": "Repeated hydration/dehydration cycles promote nitrile hydration and subsequent hydrolysis to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "A non-vent alternative is plausible in wet-dry systems where concentration, local acidity/basicity, and repeated heating can gradually hydrolyze aminonitriles. Mineral crusts also help retain the substrate long enough for slow conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Mineral-interface cyanamide guanidination of ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["apatite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "apatite", "aqueous": true, "other": "phosphate-rich concentrated microphase or mineral interface; repeated heating/cooling without complete desiccation"}, "mechanism": "The distal side-chain amine adds to cyanamide to form an amidino intermediate that tautomerizes to the guanidino group of arginine.", "reasoning": "Cyanamide is a plausible prebiotic amidinylating reagent. In a partially hydrated mineral-interface microphase, ornithine can be concentrated and oriented, while its alpha-amino group is partly deactivated by zwitterionic interactions relative to the distal side-chain amine. Regioselectivity is not guaranteed, but arginine formation is chemically plausible."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Dry-state cyanamide guanidination of ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["silica"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silica", "aqueous": false, "other": "geothermal dry-down residue or hot-spring margin with low water activity"}, "mechanism": "Low water activity concentrates ornithine and cyanamide, promoting amidination of the terminal amine to generate the guanidino functionality of arginine.", "reasoning": "Dry-down conditions help overcome the entropic cost of guanidination by strongly concentrating reactants. This route is chemically direct and consistent with cyanamide-driven condensation chemistry, although side reactions and imperfect regioselectivity remain real uncertainties."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Stepwise dinitrile route with vent hydrolysis and interfacial guanidination", "description": "Acrolein is made by aldol chemistry, trapped as 4-cyanobutanal, con