{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T18:45:38.909340", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Arginine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.53\n- Feedback: The network is topologically adequate and connected, and the overall strategy of building ornithine then guanidinating it to arginine is coherent. However, the supplied ground-truth balance check is a major liability: only 4 of 12 reactions are balanced as written, rxn_004 needs a coefficient of 2 on H2, and 7 reactions are unbalanced. Specifically, rxn_001, rxn_003, rxn_005, and rxn_007 each omit H2O as a product; rxn_008 likely omits a second HCN as well as H2O; and both rxn_009 and rxn_010 should be written as mol_011 + 2 H2O -> mol_012 + NH3. Because every route depends on an invalid ornithine-forming hydrolysis step, the target is not convincingly achieved as written. Chemically, the late cyanamide guanidination steps are among the stronger parts of the network, and P6 is the least problematic pathway because it avoids selective mono-reduction of a dinitrile and uses the more plausible dry-state guanidination. To become competitive, the network needs corrected stoichiometry, explicit catalysts and conditions for the hydrothermal reduction and reductive amination steps, a clearer geochemical replacement for the current Biochemical label on rxn_011, and stronger support for controlling acrolein side reactions and the intended structure in rxn_006.\n- Issues: ['The route is strategically coherent, but the written chemistry is not yet valid enough. rxn_001 and rxn_003 each need H2O as a product, rxn_004 should explicitly use 2 H2 and still requires selective mono-reduction of a dinitrile, and rxn_009 is invalid as written because ornithine nitrile hydrolysis should consume 2 H2O and release NH3. rxn_011 is chemically plausible, but the Biochemical label is too vague for a prebiotic setting.', 'This is one of the better versions of the stepwise dinitrile route because dry-state cyanamide guanidination is environmentally credible. However, rxn_001 and rxn_003 still omit H2O, rxn_004 needs 2 H2 and remains selective, and rxn_009 is a conservation violation unless rewritten with 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as a product. The final guanidination is plausible, but the pathway still depends on an invalid ornithine-forming step.', 'The backbone logic remains sound, but the surface-hydrolysis variant is weakened by both bookkeeping and environmental definition. rxn_001 and rxn_003 need H2O as product, rxn_004 should be written with 2 H2 and faces selectivity issues, and rxn_010 is invalid as written because nitrile hydrolysis to ornithine should consume 2 H2O and release NH3. rxn_011 is plausible chemistry, but its Biochemical setting is underspecified.', 'Dry-state guanidination helps the late-stage chemistry, but the pathway still fails on a required upstream conservation step. rxn_001 and rxn_003 omit H2O, rxn_004 needs 2 H2 and selective mono-reduction remains challenging, and rxn_010 must be rewritten with 2 H2O in and NH3 out to represent ornithine formation. As written, arginine is not convincingly reached.', 'This route is fairly coherent and avoids the hardest selectivity issue of the dinitrile path, but several steps are underspecified. rxn_001, rxn_005, and rxn_007 each need H2O as a product, rxn_006 needs stronger support that it forms the C-C extended 5-oxo-2-aminopentanenitrile rather than an N-alkylated aza-Michael adduct, and rxn_009 must consume 2 H2O and release NH3. rxn_011 again needs a clearer geochemical setting than the current Biochemical label.', 'This is the strongest pathway in the set. It avoids selective mono-reduction of a dinitrile and ends with the better-defined dry-state cyanamide step, but it still has major correctness problems: rxn_001 and rxn_005 need H2O as product, rxn_007 also needs H2O as product, rxn_006 needs stronger evidence for the intended C-C extension, and rxn_009 is invalid unless written with 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as a product. The route remains unapproved until ornithine formation is corrected.', 'The aminoacetonitrile chain-extension logic is reasonable, but the implementation is incomplete. rxn_001, rxn_005, and rxn_007 each omit H2O, rxn_006 still needs support for C-C extension versus N-alkylation, and rxn_010 is invalid as written because hydrolysis to ornithine should take 2 H2O and release NH3. The final guanidination chemistry is plausible, but the Biochemical label on rxn_011 is not an adequate environmental specification.', 'This is a relatively strong surface-terminated version of the aminoacetonitrile route, mainly because the final dry-state guanidination is plausible. Even so, rxn_001 and rxn_005 need H2O as product, rxn_007 also needs H2O as product, rxn_006 needs stronger evidence for the intended carbon-skeleton construction, and rxn_010 must be rewritten as hydrolysis with 2 H2O in and NH3 out. The target is therefore not securely achieved as written.', 'This shortcut route is the weakest. rxn_008 is not conserved as written and likely needs a second HCN plus H2O, or else it should be decomposed into explicit Michael-addition and Strecker steps. rxn_004 still needs 2 H2 and selective reduction support, rxn_009 must consume 2 H2O and release NH3, and rxn_011 still lacks a clearly prebiotic environmental definition. Too much chemistry is compressed into one poorly specified step.', 'Dry-state guanidination improves the final step, but the abbreviated upstream chemistry remains too weak. rxn_008 is mis-specified and likely needs an extra HCN plus H2O, rxn_004 needs 2 H2 and still requires selective reduction evidence, and rxn_010 is invalid unless hydrolysis is written with 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as a product. As written, the route does not securely deliver ornithine before guanidination.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Arginine is built by first forming ornithine, because guanidination of a pre-existing terminal amine with cyanamide is cleaner than installing the guanidino group early. Succinaldehyde is used as the minimal C4 dialdehyde feedstock because it maps directly onto the ornithine side chain. The design avoids selective mono-reduction of dinitriles by generating the nitrile only at the alpha position during the Strecker step, after the terminal amine precursor is already established. Two upstream surface-geochemical branches feed the 4-aminobutanal pool: a wetter reductive monoamination branch and a drier 1-pyrroline reservoir that reopens on hydration. 4-Aminobutanal is then trapped by Strecker chemistry in either evaporitic pools or tidal-flat films to give ornithine nitrile. Three surface hydrolysis regimes convert the nitrile to ornithine, and two low-water cyanamide amidination settings convert ornithine to arginine, yielding ten representative pathways.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-pyrroline\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Delta(1)-pyrroline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Wet-field reductive monoamination of succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS, FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"subaerial geothermal ponds supplied with H2-bearing volcanic gases\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde condenses with NH3 to an imine, and mineral-activated H2 reduces that imine to the amine while the second aldehyde remains available. The product belongs to the open-chain 4-aminobutanal pool that can later cyclize or be trapped chemically.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Surface geothermal fields combine H2-bearing gases with metal sulfides capable of reducing imines. Monoamination of a dialdehyde will not be perfectly selective, but under wetter conditions the productive open-chain amino aldehyde pool can persist long enough to be trapped in later chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-film reductive cyclization of succinaldehyde to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS, FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"intermittently drying geothermal mineral films with H2-bearing gases; 2 H2O are produced overall\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"After initial reductive amination at one aldehyde, the new amine attacks the remaining aldehyde intramolecularly and dehydration gives the cyclic imine 1-pyrroline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The same first monoamination, performed in drier films, should strongly favor intramolecular capture of the second aldehyde. Explicitly including 1-pyrroline prevents hiding this major sink and makes the network chemically more realistic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic ring opening of 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cooler pond, shoreline, or tidal wet phases\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The imine is hydrated to a carbinolamine and the ring opens to regenerate the open-chain amino aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"1-Pyrroline is not a dead end: hydration during cooler wet phases reopens it to 4-aminobutanal, reconnecting the cyclized pool to Strecker chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Evaporitic-pool Strecker synthesis of ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"evaporitic pools with high cyanide and ammonium concentration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"4-Aminobutanal forms an imine or iminium with NH3, cyanide adds at the carbonyl-derived center, and proton transfer gives the alpha-aminonitrile with net loss of H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Evaporitic pools are strong candidates for classical Strecker chemistry because HCN and NH3 can concentrate as waters shrink. Trapping 4-aminobutanal as ornithine nitrile preserves the five-carbon skeleton and installs the alpha-amino acid precursor cleanly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Tidal-flat Strecker synthesis of ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-325K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"salt-rich tidal-flat films with periodic wetting and drying; productive chemistry assumed in mineral-shaded or brine-rich microenvironments\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same Strecker sequence occurs in thin films: imine formation, cyanide addition, and proton transfer generate the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Tidal-flat thin films offer the same Strecker chemistry under repeated salting-out and wetting-drying. UV is a liability, so productive chemistry is most plausible in mineral-shaded or brine-rich films, but this remains a credible alternative surface setting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hot-spring hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic sulfur-rich volcanic hot springs, pH about 2-5; 2 H2O are consumed overall and NH3 is released\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted nitrile hydration gives the amide and then the carboxylic acid, with NH3 released overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid catalysis in sulfur-rich hot springs is the quickest surface route from aminonitrile to amino acid. The net hydrolysis consumes 2 H2O and releases NH3, giving ornithine without invoking any selective nitrile reduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Silica-film geothermal hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-355K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"silica-rich sinter surfaces with repeated heating-cooling; 2 H2O are consumed overall and NH3 is released\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thin water films on silica permit stepwise nitrile hydration through the amide to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Silica-rich geothermal sinters retain thin water films and concentrate adsorbed organics. Although slower than strong acid or base pathways, extended heating-cooling cycles make a neutral-film hydrolysis branch plausible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline evaporitic hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"alkaline evaporitic pools, pH about 8-10, with wet-dry cycling; 2 H2O are consumed overall and NH3 is released\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-assisted hydration and repeated concentration-deconcentration drive conversion of the nitrile through the amide to the carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alkaline evaporitic pools provide base-assisted hydration and long residence times through wet-dry cycling. This is slower than the acidic branch but geochemically credible and still gives the correct ornithine plus NH3 hydrolysis stoichiometry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Silica dry-down guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"evaporitic or geothermal dry-down with low water activity\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal delta-amine of ornithine attacks cyanamide, and proton transfer plus tautomerization yields the guanidino group of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanamide is one of the most plausible prebiotic amidinating agents. Low-water silica surfaces suppress its wasteful hydrolysis and favor attack by the terminal side-chain amine of ornithine, giving the guanidino group of arginine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Interfacial-film guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-345K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"organic-mineral interfacial films on geothermal shorelines; concentrated solutes with little free water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Interfacial concentration brings ornithine and cyanamide into close contact, enabling the same amidination and tautomerization sequence without full bulk-water dilution.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A concentrated organic-mineral film is a second credible low-water niche for the same cyanamide chemistry. It avoids requiring full desiccation while still favoring amidination over bulk-water hydrolysis.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-evaporitic-acidic-dry route\",\n      \"description\": \"Wetter NiS/FeS-mediated monoamination is followed by evaporitic-pool Strecker capture, acidic hot-spring hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile, and silica dry-down guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-evaporitic-silica-film route\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct monoamination feeds evaporitic-pool Strecker chemistry, silica-film hydrolysis, and interfacial-film guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-evaporitic-alkaline route\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct monoamination is coupled to evaporitic-pool Strecker capture, alkaline evaporitic hydrolysis, and silica dry-down guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-tidal-acidic-interfacial route\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct monoamination feeds tidal-flat Strecker chemistry, acidic hot-spring hydrolysis, and interfacial-film guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-tidal-silica-film-dry route\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct monoamination is followed by tidal-flat Strecker synthesis, silica-film hydrolysis, and dry-down guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-tidal-alkaline-interfacial route\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct monoamination is combined with tidal-flat Strecker chemistry, alkaline evaporitic hydrolysis, and interfacial-film guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclized-evaporitic-acidic-dry route\",\n      \"description\": \"A drier 1-pyrroline reservoir is reopened by hydration, then captured by evaporitic-pool Strecker chemistry, acidic hydrolysis, and dry-down guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclized-evaporitic-silica-film route\",\n      \"description\": \"The 1-pyrroline branch reopens to 4-aminobutanal, then proceeds through evaporitic-pool Strecker chemistry, silica-film hydrolysis, and interfacial-film guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclized-tidal-silica-film-dry route\",\n      \"description\": \"The cyclized reservoir is reopened during wet phases, trapped by tidal-flat Strecker chemistry, hydrolyzed on silica-rich geothermal films, and guanidinated during dry-down.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclized-tidal-alkaline-interfacial route\",\n      \"description\": \"The 1-pyrroline branch reconnects to tidal-flat Strecker chemistry, then alkaline evaporitic hydrolysis and interfacial-film guanidination deliver arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol