m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Arginine is built by first forming ornithine, because guanidination of a pre-existing terminal amine with cyanamide is cleaner than installing the guanidino group early. Succinaldehyde is used as the minimal C4 dialdehyde feedstock because it maps directly onto the ornithine side chain. The design avoids selective mono-reduction of dinitriles by generating the nitrile only at the alpha position during the Strecker step, after the terminal amine precursor is already established. Two upstream surface-geochemical branches feed the 4-aminobutanal pool: a wetter reductive monoamination branch and a drier 1-pyrroline reservoir that reopens on hydration. 4-Aminobutanal is then trapped by Strecker chemistry in either evaporitic pools or tidal-flat films to give ornithine nitrile. Three surface hydrolysis regimes convert the nitrile to ornithine, and two low-water cyanamide amidination settings convert ornithine to arginine, yielding ten representative pathways.\n\nMOLECULES (11 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C4H6O2 (succinaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C4H9NO (4-aminobutanal) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C4H7N (