y driven step, and rxn_006 also needs 2 H2O.', 'This is the strongest pathway in the set because the cyclized reservoir acknowledges likely upstream speciation and the silica-based late stage is reasonably coherent. Remaining concerns are the stoichiometric fixes in rxn_002 and rxn_007, reversible ring opening in rxn_003, uncertain Strecker trapping in rxn_004, and incomplete evidence for selective rxn_009 guanidination.', 'Reservoir logic helps upstream, but tidal-flat Strecker conditions are a bit less persuasive than evaporitic concentration for trapping the open-chain aminoaldehyde. rxn_002 should give 2 H2O, rxn_007 needs 2 H2O, and transfer from tidal films to silica-film hydrolysis and dry-down should be made explicit.', 'The dry-to-wet-to-dry surface narrative is plausible, but the chemistry is pulled down by equilibrium ring opening, less favorable alkaline hydrolysis, and selectivity in interfacial cyanamide amidination. rxn_002 should release 2 H2O and rxn_008 should consume 2 H2O.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Use a plausible hydrothermal C4 precursor, succinic acid, rather than force a poorly constrained C1/C2 build. H2-driven Fe-Ni reduction gives succinaldehyde, which enters nitrogen chemistry by direct reductive amination or by cyclizing reductive amination to 1-pyrroline, an explicit reservoir for the unstable 4-aminobutanal/1-pyrroline pair. A single main hydrothermal-to-surface transfer is then used because HCN capture and low-water cyanamide chemistry are better supported in cooler surface films. Surface Strecker chemistry gives ornithine nitrile, after which the network branches into ornithine-first and nitrile-first finishes: ornithine nitrile can be hydrolyzed in acidic pools or silica wet-dry films, then guanidinated with cyanamide or dicyand