null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reduction of succinic acid to succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Subseafloor H2-rich alkaline vent pores; net stoichiometry is succinic acid + 2 H2 -> succinaldehyde + 2 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Adsorbed carboxylates undergo H2-driven reduction on Fe-Ni sulfides and alloys, plausibly via transient surface acyl or formyl intermediates, to give the dialdehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reduced C4 dicarboxylates are more plausible vent organics than free dialdehydes. The dialdehyde-forming reduction is speculative, but H2-rich Fe-Ni sulfide and alloy systems are among the few prebiotic settings capable of supplying both the needed reducing power and surface activation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct mineral-catalyzed reductive amination of succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH3-rich vent mixing zone; short residence times help limit overreduction and diamination\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde forms an imine with ammonia and is reduced by surface-activated H2, leaving the second aldehyde intact.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct mono-reductive amination gives the shortest route to 4-aminobutanal, but the step competes with cyclization to 1-pyrroline and possible overreduction. It is therefore included as a plausible branch rather than assumed to dominate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclizing reductive amination of succinaldehyde to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH3-rich vent mixing zone; net stoichiometry is succinaldehyde + NH3 + H2 -> 1-pyrroline + 2 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde is reductively aminated; the resulting aminoaldehyde then cyclizes onto the second aldehyde and dehydrates to the cyclic imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyclization is intrinsically favored once an aminoaldehyde is generated, so explicit inclusion of 1-pyrroline captures the expected speciation better than treating 4-aminobutanal as permanently open-chain. This branch likely represents a realistic reservoir during transport into cooler surface waters.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic ring opening of 1-pyrroline to 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Vent-discharge margin or prebiotic pool; reversible ring-opening equilibrium during cooling and dilution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine hydration followed by ring opening regenerates the open-chain aminoaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"1-Pyrroline and 4-aminobutanal are in reversible aqueous equilibrium. The ring-opened form need not accumulate strongly; it only has to be regenerated often enough for cyanide trapping in the next step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker capture of 4-aminobutanal to ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Evaporitic pool or tidal-film concentration of HCN and NH3; rapid trapping of open-chain aminoaldehyde required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Strecker addition: aldehyde imination with ammonia followed by cyanide capture gives the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker capture is the most chemically direct way to convert the remaining aldehyde into the alpha-amino acid skeleton. High NH3 and HCN concentration and cooling are important because 4-aminobutanal is short-lived and otherwise re-cyclizes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic pool hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic volcanic hot-spring pool or sulfate-rich film; net stoichiometry is ornithine nitrile + 2 H2O -> ornithine + NH3\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration to amide and then carboxylic acid with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acidic wet-heating is the more defensible nitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis mode here. This step explicitly represents the net transformation aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> amino acid + NH3, avoiding the under-specified hydrolyses that weakened earlier networks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Silica-film wet-dry hydrolysis of ornithine nitrile to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-355K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica film\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Silica-sinter wet-dry cycling in a subaerial geothermal field; net stoichiometry is ornithine nitrile + 2 H2O -> ornithine + NH3\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Progressive nitrile hydration occurs over repeated wetting and concentration cycles on silica films.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Silica-rich wet-dry films offer a second, more environmentally continuous surface hydrolysis route after Strecker capture. This branch is probably slower than rxn_006 but gentler and better aligned with repeated concentration and rehydration cycles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-state cyanamide guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\", \"apatite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica/apatite surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Low-water geothermal film, mildly basic; repeated drying suppresses cyanamide hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Terminal amine addition to cyanamide followed by proton transfer and tautomerization furnishes the guanidino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanamide is a credible prebiotic amidinyl source under low-water conditions. Direct guanidination of ornithine is chemically plausible on mineral films, but side-chain versus alpha-amine selectivity remains a genuine uncertainty, so this branch is retained as one option rather than the sole finish.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-state dimerization of cyanamide to dicyandiamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Dry-down on evaporitic or geothermal crust; net stoichiometry is 2 cyanamide -> dicyandiamide\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal condensation of two cyanamide molecules to N-cyanoguanidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Warm dry surfaces naturally convert part of a cyanamide inventory into dicyandiamide. Including this reaction captures a realistic surface-aging process and provides an alternative amidino donor without requiring a wholly different feedstock class.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Dicyandiamide-mediated guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\", \"apatite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica/apatite surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Low-water reheating of dicyandiamide-rich film; released cyanamide can recycle\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amidino transfer from dicyandiamide to the terminal amine expels cyanamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Dicyandiamide is less labile than cyanamide and can act as a buffered amidino donor in reheated low-water films. The step is slower than direct cyanamide addition and still faces regioselectivity challenges, but it gives a chemically distinct surface route to arginine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-state cyanamide guanidination of ornithine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Drying silica film with limited water activity; nitrile is retained during amidination\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The side-chain amine adds to cyanamide and tautomerizes to the amidino product while the aminonitrile remains intact.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Guanidination before nitrile hydrolysis may improve selectivity because the alpha-amino group of an aminonitrile is electronically damped by the adjacent nitrile, leaving the terminal side-chain amine relatively more nucleophilic. This is one of the stronger late-stage branches in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydrolysis of arginine nitrile to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic hot-spring rehydration after dry-down; net stoichiometry is arginine nitrile + 2 H2O -> arginine + NH3\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the nitrile to the carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Once the guanidino group is installed, ordinary aminonitrile hydrolysis completes the amino acid. Moderate acidic surface rehydration is preferred because the guanidino group is robust enough to survive it, whereas prolonged hotter conditions risk broader decomposition.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminoaldehyde / acidic hydrolysis / cyanamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Shortest ornithine-first route: direct hydrothermal aminoaldehyde formation, surface Strecker capture, acidic hydrolysis to ornithine, then cyanamide guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminoaldehyde / acidic hydrolysis / dicyandiamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Branched ornithine-first route: direct aminoaldehyde formation and Strecker capture are followed by a parallel surface dry-down that converts cyanamide to dicyandiamide before final amidino transfer to ornithine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminoaldehyde / silica hydrolysis / cyanamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct aminoaldehyde branch with silica-sinter wet-dry hydrolysis instead of bulk acidic hydrolysis before cyanamide guanidination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminoaldehyde / silica hydrolysis / dicyandiamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct aminoaldehyde branch plus si